#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""session-resolve — turn ANY human- or agent-shaped selector into ONE target.

    session-resolve show <selector> [--json]
    session-resolve resolve <selector> [--json]      # alias of `show`

READ-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION. This module never runs a tmux write verb. The only
tmux subcommands it will ever pass to a runner are the three listed in
`TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS`, and `_assert_read_only` enforces that at the seam
rather than asserting it in a comment — `select-window`, `send-keys`,
`switch-client`, `detach-client`, `kill-*` cannot leave this file. Write verbs
are a later PR.


🔴 A MEASURED REFUTATION OF THE ORIGINAL BRIEF — READ BEFORE CHANGING THE JOIN
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The brief for this module said to consume `session-manager --json --lean` and to
join on `(host, session, window_id)`, never on `window_index`. Those two
instructions are incompatible, and the incompatibility is measured, not argued:

    $ python3 scripts/session-manager --json --lean --no-ch --host workbench
      lean_row_fields: [... 'session', 'window_index', ...]      # no window_id
    $ python3 scripts/session-manager --json      --no-ch --host workbench
      row keys:        [... 'session', 'window_id', 'window_index', ...]

`--lean` DROPS `window_id`. It carries `window_index` only, as a string ("1").
So the lean view cannot supply the join key the brief (correctly) mandates, and
the only way to use it would be the `window_index` join the brief (correctly)
forbids — indexes renumber when windows close.

Resolution: this module consumes the FULL `--json` view. That view is a strict
superset of the lean one for every field the brief asked for (`waiting_probable`,
`waiting_signals`, `unsent_prompt`, `age_secs`, `age_source`, `runtime`,
`claude_session_id`, `status`), so nothing is lost and the mandated join key is
present. `SM_ARGV` pins the flags; `test_session_resolve.py` pins the absence of
`--lean` so a future edit cannot quietly reintroduce the unjoinable view.

The join was then verified end-to-end rather than assumed. Measured 2026-08-19 on
the workbench: 59 session-manager rows, 59 distinct `(session, window_id)` pairs,
48 registry records of which 24 carry a `tmux` address — and **24 of 24 joined**,
zero unmatched, zero duplicate addresses. 35 windows had no registry record. That
is normal, and it is reported as a coverage count, never as an error or a zero.


THE FOUR SOURCES, AND THE ONE KEY THAT JOINS THEM
-------------------------------------------------
The join key is `(host, session, window_id)`. `window_id` is tmux's `@N`, which
is stable for the life of a window; `window_index` is a display ordinal that
RENUMBERS when a neighbour closes. Joining on the index silently reattaches a
harness record to whichever window slid into the vacated slot.

  1. tmux — `list-panes` / `list-windows` / `list-clients`. Supplies host,
     session, window_index, window_id (@N), pane_id (%N), cwd, pane title, and
     which windows are active.

  2. The slot table — `scripts/tmux-scratch-slots.sh`, entries shaped
     `scratch2:G:#d79921:Gold` = session:key:colour:codename. PARSED, never
     re-hardcoded: the tmux bindings are generated from this same file at
     home-manager build time, so a private copy would drift silently. A session
     with no slot entry (`datapacket-talos`) has NO hotkey and NO codename —
     its label falls back to the cwd, and none is invented.

  3. The harness session registry — `~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json`, joined via
     its `tmux` field, which is `session:@window_id.%pane_id`.
     🔴 Its timestamps are epoch MILLISECOND INTEGERS, not ISO strings. A parser
     that handles only ISO returns None for every record and reads exactly like
     an absent field. `parse_epoch_ms` accepts int/float only and reports a
     reason for anything else — a string timestamp is UNMEASURED, never 0 and
     never silently dropped.

  4. session-manager — the observed status bucket, `waiting_probable` and its
     signals, `unsent_prompt`, `age_secs`/`age_source`, `runtime`,
     `claude_session_id`.
     🔴 CONSUMED, NEVER RE-DERIVED. Its waiting detector has a real
     false-positive history; a second copy of the predicate would regenerate
     every one of those bugs. This module reads its output and does not
     reimplement one line of it.


🔴 TWO STATUSES, NEVER COLLAPSED
--------------------------------
`harness_status` (registry) and `waiting_probable` (session-manager) are
DIFFERENT MEASUREMENTS OF DIFFERENT THINGS, and they disagree. Measured at one
instant: session-manager reported 3 waiting windows; the registry view reported
1, a disjoint one, and called one of session-manager's three `idle`. Zero
overlap.

The registry `status` means "this harness process is waiting on a turn". The
session-manager `waiting_probable` means "the pane TEXT looks like it is asking
a human". Both are carried, under those distinct names. They are never merged,
never summed, and NO MAPPING BETWEEN THEM IS WRITTEN — the correct mapping is
unknown, and inventing one now would repeat the `blocked_on_me` mistake this
repo just finished fixing.


🔴 NULL IS NOT ZERO
-------------------
An unreachable host, an absent registry directory, a failed `gh`, a tmux call
that errored — each yields None plus a `*_status` of `unmeasured:<reason>`, never
0, never False, never an absent key. `unmeasured()` is the only way to spell it.
A measured-but-empty result (tmux answered, and no client is attached) is a real
zero and is reported as one; the two are distinguishable by the status field.

NO SILENT CAPS: anything dropped is counted and named in `dropped`.

ON-DISK ARTIFACTS: this module creates NONE. The `gh` PR lookup is opt-in and
memoised in a plain dict for the life of the process, deliberately not written to
`~/.cache` — see `test_session_resolve.py::test_module_creates_no_on_disk_
artifacts` and `scripts/claude-hooks/tests/test_on_disk_artifact_names.py` for
why an on-disk name is a liability that no behavioural test can see move.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as _dt
import glob
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Any, Callable, Optional

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Constants — every literal a caller or a test might need to name.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: tmux `-F` formats. Kept as module constants so the tests can build fixture
#: output from the SAME format string the production code parses; a fixture
#: built from a hand-copied format is a fixture that stops matching silently.
PANE_FORMAT = (
    "#{host}\x1f#{session_name}\x1f#{window_index}\x1f#{window_id}"
    "\x1f#{pane_id}\x1f#{pane_current_path}\x1f#{pane_title}"
)
WINDOW_FORMAT = "#{session_name}\x1f#{window_id}\x1f#{window_active}"
CLIENT_FORMAT = (
    "#{client_tty}\x1f#{client_termname}\x1f#{client_width}"
    "\x1f#{client_height}\x1f#{client_session}\x1f#{client_flags}"
)
FIELD_SEP = "\x1f"

#: 🔴 The ONLY tmux subcommands this module may ever run. Enforced by
#: `_assert_read_only`, not by review. A write verb added to a call site raises.
TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS = ("list-panes", "list-windows", "list-clients")

#: 🔴 session-manager flags. `--lean` is deliberately ABSENT — see the module
#: docstring's refutation; the lean view does not carry `window_id`.
SM_ARGV = ("--json", "--no-ch")

#: A client whose TERM is tmux's own is, structurally, a client running INSIDE
#: tmux — i.e. a `display-popup` overlay created by the Alt-key bindings, not a
#: terminal emulator. Measured: the base terminal reports `alacritty` at 312x64;
#: the two popups report `tmux-256color` at 247x49 and 195x37. The discriminator
#: is the TERM NAME, not the size — sizes are recorded as evidence only, because
#: a resized terminal must not be reclassified as a popup.
POPUP_TERM_PREFIX = "tmux"

DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/sessions")
DEFAULT_SLOT_TABLE = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                                  "tmux-scratch-slots.sh")
SLOT_ARRAY_NAME = "SCRATCH_SLOTS"

#: Modifier tokens stripped from a hotkey selector so `Alt+Shift+G` and `G` are
#: the same request. The KEY ITSELF KEEPS ITS CASE — `g` is grove, `G` is Gold.
HOTKEY_SEPARATOR = "+"

SOURCE_TMUX = "tmux"
SOURCE_SLOTS = "slot-table"
SOURCE_REGISTRY = "harness-registry"
SOURCE_SM = "session-manager"
ALL_SOURCES = (SOURCE_TMUX, SOURCE_SLOTS, SOURCE_REGISTRY, SOURCE_SM)

STATUS_RESOLVED = "resolved"
STATUS_AMBIGUOUS = "ambiguous"
STATUS_UNMATCHED = "unmatched"

SELECTOR_CODENAME = "codename"
SELECTOR_HOTKEY = "hotkey"
SELECTOR_SESSION_INDEX = "session:index"
SELECTOR_ADDRESS = "session:@window_id"
SELECTOR_WINDOW_ID = "window_id"
SELECTOR_HARNESS_NAME = "harness_name"
SELECTOR_CWD = "cwd"
SELECTOR_SESSION = "session"
ALL_SELECTOR_KINDS = (
    SELECTOR_CODENAME, SELECTOR_HOTKEY, SELECTOR_SESSION_INDEX,
    SELECTOR_ADDRESS, SELECTOR_WINDOW_ID, SELECTOR_HARNESS_NAME,
    SELECTOR_CWD, SELECTOR_SESSION,
)

LABEL_SOURCE_SLOT = "slot"
LABEL_SOURCE_CWD = "cwd"
LABEL_SOURCE_SESSION = "session"

_UNMEASURED_PREFIX = "unmeasured:"
MEASURED_OK = "ok"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 🔴 THE PRESENCE TRICHOTOMY — three states, three tokens, for EVERY optional
# source. Two tokens is the bug: with only present/absent, "the registry could
# not be read" and "the registry was read and this window has no record" collapse
# into the same empty field, and an operator goes hunting a broken registry that
# is working perfectly. Measured: 24 of 59 windows carried a harness record, so
# ABSENT is the MAJORITY case and is a real measurement.
#
# The same trichotomy binds every other optional source — the session-manager
# row, the git branch, and above all the `gh` PR lookup, where "no open PR for
# this branch" (measured: the work is done) and "gh failed or was not run"
# (unmeasured) mean opposite things to anyone deciding what to do next.
#
# Each carrier is a `<source>_presence` field holding exactly one of these, and
# `<source>_measurement` carries the REASON whenever the value is UNMEASURED.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
PRESENCE_PRESENT = "present"
PRESENCE_ABSENT = "absent"
PRESENCE_UNMEASURED = "unmeasured"
ALL_PRESENCE_STATES = (PRESENCE_PRESENT, PRESENCE_ABSENT, PRESENCE_UNMEASURED)


def unmeasured(reason: str) -> str:
    """The ONLY spelling of a not-measured status. Never returns a bare 0/None.

    A status string is `ok` or `unmeasured:<reason>`; `is_unmeasured` is the only
    predicate that reads it, so a typo in one place cannot make a failure read as
    a success anywhere else.
    """
    if not reason:
        raise ValueError("unmeasured() requires a reason — a bare 'unmeasured' "
                         "is indistinguishable from a measured zero")
    return _UNMEASURED_PREFIX + reason


def is_unmeasured(status: Optional[str]) -> bool:
    return bool(status) and str(status).startswith(_UNMEASURED_PREFIX)


class ReadOnlyViolation(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when a tmux write verb reaches the subprocess seam."""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Subprocess seam — the ONE impure entry point, replaced wholesale in tests.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _assert_read_only(argv) -> None:
    """🔴 Structural enforcement of the READ-ONLY contract.

    Checks the tmux SUBCOMMAND against an allowlist rather than scanning for
    banned words: a denylist of `select-window`/`send-keys`/… is walkable by any
    verb nobody thought to ban, and this module's whole promise is that no such
    verb exists. An allowlist fails closed.
    """
    argv = list(argv)
    if not argv or os.path.basename(argv[0]) != "tmux":
        return
    for token in argv[1:]:
        if token.startswith("-"):
            continue
        if token not in TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS:
            raise ReadOnlyViolation(
                f"session-resolve is READ-ONLY: refusing tmux subcommand "
                f"{token!r}; allowed: {', '.join(TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS)}")
        return


def _default_runner(argv, timeout: int = 20):
    """Run argv, return (rc, stdout, stderr). Never raises on non-zero rc."""
    _assert_read_only(argv)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=timeout, check=False)
    except FileNotFoundError as exc:
        return 127, "", f"not found: {exc}"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", f"timed out after {timeout}s"
    except OSError as exc:  # pragma: no cover - defensive
        return 1, "", str(exc)
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr


@dataclass
class Sources:
    """Every impure input, injectable. Nothing here touches the live machine
    when all four raw fields are supplied — which is how the whole test suite
    runs, and why `conftest`-style raisers can prove it.

    A `*_raw` / `*_payload` field that is not None is used VERBATIM and the
    corresponding subprocess is never spawned.
    """
    host: str = "workbench"
    runner: Callable[..., Any] = _default_runner

    panes_raw: Optional[str] = None
    windows_raw: Optional[str] = None
    clients_raw: Optional[str] = None

    #: None -> read DEFAULT_SLOT_TABLE from disk. A str is the file's TEXT.
    slot_table_text: Optional[str] = None
    slot_table_path: str = DEFAULT_SLOT_TABLE

    registry_dir: str = DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR
    #: None -> scan registry_dir. A list is the already-loaded records.
    registry_records: Optional[list] = None

    #: None -> shell out to session-manager. A dict is its parsed JSON.
    sm_payload: Optional[dict] = None
    sm_script: str = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                                  "session-manager")

    #: Opt-in. The `gh` call is the slowest and least reliable source, so it is
    #: OFF unless asked for, and its failure degrades to UNMEASURED — never to
    #: "no PR", which would read as a measured absence of a blocked PR.
    want_pr: bool = False
    git_runner: Optional[Callable[..., Any]] = None
    gh_runner: Optional[Callable[..., Any]] = None
    #: Process-lifetime memo. Deliberately in memory: this module writes no
    #: on-disk artifact, so there is no cache filename to rot or to rename.
    pr_cache: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    branch_cache: dict = field(default_factory=dict)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 2. The slot table
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def parse_slot_table(text: str) -> dict:
    """Parse `SCRATCH_SLOTS=( "session:key:colour:name" ... )` -> {session: slot}.

    Parses the ARRAY, not the whole file: the table is a sourced shell fragment
    whose comments contain `scratch4` and `$mod+Shift+V` in prose, so a
    line-oriented grep for `scratch` picks up documentation as data.
    """
    slots: dict = {}
    if not text:
        return slots
    in_array = False
    for line in text.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not in_array:
            if stripped.startswith(SLOT_ARRAY_NAME + "=("):
                in_array = True
            continue
        if stripped.startswith(")"):
            break
        if not stripped or stripped.startswith("#"):
            continue
        entry = stripped.strip().strip(",").strip()
        if entry.startswith('"') and entry.endswith('"'):
            entry = entry[1:-1]
        elif entry.startswith("'") and entry.endswith("'"):
            entry = entry[1:-1]
        parts = entry.split(":")
        if len(parts) != 4:
            continue
        session, key, colour, name = (p.strip() for p in parts)
        if not session:
            continue
        slots[session] = {"session": session, "hotkey": key,
                          "colour": colour, "codename": name}
    return slots


def load_slots(src: Sources) -> tuple:
    """-> (slots, status, dropped_note_or_None)."""
    if src.slot_table_text is not None:
        return parse_slot_table(src.slot_table_text), MEASURED_OK, None
    try:
        with open(src.slot_table_path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {}, unmeasured(f"slot-table-absent:{src.slot_table_path}"), None
    except OSError as exc:
        return {}, unmeasured(f"slot-table-unreadable:{exc}"), None
    return parse_slot_table(text), MEASURED_OK, None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 1. tmux
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _split_rows(raw: str, arity: int):
    """-> (rows, malformed_count). A short row is DROPPED and COUNTED, never
    padded — a padded row would join against the wrong window."""
    rows, malformed = [], 0
    for line in (raw or "").splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        parts = line.split(FIELD_SEP)
        if len(parts) < arity:
            malformed += 1
            continue
        rows.append(parts[:arity])
    return rows, malformed


def parse_panes(raw: str) -> tuple:
    rows, malformed = _split_rows(raw, 7)
    panes = []
    for host, session, w_index, w_id, p_id, path, title in rows:
        panes.append({"tmux_host": host, "session": session,
                      "window_index": w_index, "window_id": w_id,
                      "pane_id": p_id, "cwd": path or None,
                      "pane_title": title or None})
    return panes, malformed


def parse_windows(raw: str) -> tuple:
    """-> ({(session, window_id): active_bool}, malformed)."""
    rows, malformed = _split_rows(raw, 3)
    out = {}
    for session, w_id, active in rows:
        out[(session, w_id)] = (active.strip() == "1")
    return out, malformed


def parse_clients(raw: str) -> tuple:
    """-> (clients, malformed). Each client is classified base-terminal vs popup.

    🔴 The classification is STRUCTURAL, on TERM name: a client whose TERM is
    tmux's own is by construction nested inside tmux, i.e. a `display-popup`
    overlay. Width/height are carried as EVIDENCE so an operator can see the
    measurement, but they are not the discriminator — classifying on size would
    turn a resized terminal into a popup.
    """
    rows, malformed = _split_rows(raw, 6)
    clients = []
    for tty, term, width, height, session, flags in rows:
        is_popup = term.startswith(POPUP_TERM_PREFIX)
        clients.append({
            "tty": tty, "term": term,
            "width": _int_or_none(width), "height": _int_or_none(height),
            "size": f"{width}x{height}",
            "session": session,
            "flags": [f for f in flags.split(",") if f],
            "popup": is_popup,
            "base_terminal": not is_popup,
        })
    return clients, malformed


def _int_or_none(v):
    try:
        return int(str(v).strip())
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def collect_tmux(src: Sources) -> dict:
    """Gather the three tmux reads. Any failure is UNMEASURED with a reason;
    an unreachable tmux server yields None lists, never empty ones."""
    out = {"panes": None, "windows": None, "clients": None,
           "panes_status": MEASURED_OK, "windows_status": MEASURED_OK,
           "clients_status": MEASURED_OK, "dropped": []}

    def _read(raw, argv, key, parser, arity_label):
        if raw is None:
            rc, stdout, stderr = src.runner(argv, timeout=20)
            if rc != 0:
                out[key + "_status"] = unmeasured(
                    f"tmux-{arity_label}-failed:rc={rc}:"
                    f"{(stderr or '').strip()[:120]}")
                return
            raw = stdout
        parsed, malformed = parser(raw)
        out[key] = parsed
        if malformed:
            out["dropped"].append(
                f"{malformed} malformed tmux {arity_label} line(s) dropped")

    _read(src.panes_raw,
          ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", PANE_FORMAT],
          "panes", parse_panes, "list-panes")
    _read(src.windows_raw,
          ["tmux", "list-windows", "-a", "-F", WINDOW_FORMAT],
          "windows", parse_windows, "list-windows")
    _read(src.clients_raw,
          ["tmux", "list-clients", "-F", CLIENT_FORMAT],
          "clients", parse_clients, "list-clients")
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 3. The harness session registry
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def parse_epoch_ms(value) -> tuple:
    """-> (iso_utc_str | None, reason | None).

    🔴 The registry's `startedAt` / `updatedAt` / `statusUpdatedAt` are epoch
    MILLISECOND INTEGERS (e.g. 1787117530032). A parser that accepts only ISO
    strings returns None for EVERY record and is indistinguishable from the
    field being absent — which is exactly how this field was nearly reported as
    missing from all 48 records.

    A bool is rejected explicitly: `isinstance(True, int)` is True in Python, so
    without the check `"status": true` would silently become 1970-01-01.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None, "absent"
    if isinstance(value, bool):
        return None, f"not-epoch-ms:bool:{value!r}"
    if not isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        return None, f"not-epoch-ms:{type(value).__name__}:{str(value)[:40]}"
    try:
        dt = _dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(value / 1000.0, tz=_dt.timezone.utc)
    except (OverflowError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, f"epoch-ms-out-of-range:{exc}"
    return dt.isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z"), None


def parse_tmux_address(addr) -> tuple:
    """`scratch18:@73.%73` -> (('scratch18', '@73'), '%73') | (None, None).

    The join key is (session, window_id). The pane suffix is carried for
    display but is NOT part of the key.
    """
    if not isinstance(addr, str) or ":" not in addr:
        return None, None
    session, rest = addr.split(":", 1)
    win = rest.split(".", 1)[0]
    pane = rest.split(".", 1)[1] if "." in rest else None
    if not session or not win.startswith("@"):
        return None, None
    return (session, win), pane


def load_registry(src: Sources) -> dict:
    """-> {records, by_key, status, stats, dropped}.

    A malformed JSON file is COUNTED and NAMED, never silently skipped: a
    registry half of whose files fail to parse must not look like a registry
    with half as many sessions.
    """
    out = {"records": [], "by_key": {}, "status": MEASURED_OK,
           "unparseable": 0, "unparseable_files": [], "with_tmux": 0,
           "dropped": []}

    records = src.registry_records
    if records is None:
        if not os.path.isdir(src.registry_dir):
            out["status"] = unmeasured(f"registry-absent:{src.registry_dir}")
            return out
        records = []
        for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(src.registry_dir, "*.json"))):
            try:
                with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                    obj = json.load(fh)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                out["unparseable"] += 1
                out["unparseable_files"].append(os.path.basename(path))
                continue
            if isinstance(obj, dict):
                records.append(obj)
            else:
                out["unparseable"] += 1
                out["unparseable_files"].append(os.path.basename(path))
    if out["unparseable"]:
        out["dropped"].append(
            f"{out['unparseable']} unparseable registry file(s): "
            + ", ".join(out["unparseable_files"]))

    out["records"] = records
    for obj in records:
        key, pane = parse_tmux_address(obj.get("tmux"))
        if key is None:
            continue
        out["with_tmux"] += 1
        out["by_key"].setdefault(key, []).append((obj, pane))
    return out


def shape_harness(obj: dict, pane: Optional[str]) -> dict:
    """Project one registry record into the Target's `harness` block."""
    times = {}
    for field_name in ("startedAt", "updatedAt", "statusUpdatedAt", "nameSince"):
        iso, reason = parse_epoch_ms(obj.get(field_name))
        times[field_name] = obj.get(field_name)
        times[field_name + "_iso"] = iso
        times[field_name + "_status"] = MEASURED_OK if iso else unmeasured(
            f"{field_name}:{reason}")
    return {
        "pid": obj.get("pid"),
        "session_id": obj.get("sessionId"),
        "name": obj.get("name"),
        "name_source": obj.get("nameSource"),
        "cwd": obj.get("cwd"),
        "kind": obj.get("kind"),
        "entrypoint": obj.get("entrypoint"),
        "tmux": obj.get("tmux"),
        "pane_id": pane,
        "times": times,
    }


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 4. session-manager
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def load_session_manager(src: Sources) -> dict:
    """-> {by_key, status, rows_total, rows_unjoinable, dropped}.

    🔴 CONSUMES session-manager. Re-derives NOTHING — no waiting predicate, no
    staleness bucket, no age. Every such field is copied through under the name
    session-manager gave it.

    🔴 A row WITHOUT `window_id` is recorded as UNJOINABLE and named. It is NOT
    joined on `window_index`. This is the guard that makes the `--lean` view's
    missing key loud instead of catastrophic: `--lean` rows would ALL land here,
    and the resolver reports "0 of 59 rows joinable" rather than confidently
    attaching every harness record to the wrong window.
    """
    out = {"by_key": {}, "status": MEASURED_OK, "rows_total": 0,
           "rows_unjoinable": 0, "unjoinable_reason": None, "dropped": []}

    payload = src.sm_payload
    if payload is None:
        argv = [sys.executable, src.sm_script, *SM_ARGV, "--host", src.host]
        rc, stdout, stderr = src.runner(argv, timeout=240)
        if rc != 0:
            out["status"] = unmeasured(
                f"session-manager-failed:rc={rc}:{(stderr or '').strip()[:120]}")
            return out
        try:
            payload = json.loads(stdout)
        except ValueError as exc:
            out["status"] = unmeasured(f"session-manager-bad-json:{exc}")
            return out

    hosts = (payload or {}).get("hosts")
    if not isinstance(hosts, dict):
        out["status"] = unmeasured("session-manager-no-hosts-key")
        return out

    for host_name, host_obj in hosts.items():
        if not isinstance(host_obj, dict):
            continue
        if host_obj.get("reachable") is False:
            out["dropped"].append(
                f"host {host_name} unreachable: "
                f"{host_obj.get('error') or 'no reason given'}")
            continue
        for row in host_obj.get("windows") or []:
            if not isinstance(row, dict):
                continue
            out["rows_total"] += 1
            session = row.get("session")
            window_id = row.get("window_id")
            if not session or not window_id:
                out["rows_unjoinable"] += 1
                out["unjoinable_reason"] = (
                    "row carries no window_id — refusing to join on "
                    "window_index (indexes renumber when windows close); is "
                    "this the --lean view?")
                continue
            out["by_key"].setdefault((session, window_id), []).append(
                (host_name, row))
    if out["rows_unjoinable"]:
        out["dropped"].append(
            f"{out['rows_unjoinable']} session-manager row(s) had no "
            f"window_id and were NOT joined: {out['unjoinable_reason']}")
    return out


#: Fields copied verbatim out of a session-manager row. Named as a constant so
#: the test can pin the ledger — a field added to the copy must be added here.
SM_PASSTHROUGH_FIELDS = (
    "status", "waiting_probable", "waiting_signals", "waiting_status",
    "unsent_prompt", "unsent_prompt_status", "age_secs", "age_source",
    "runtime", "claude", "busy", "task", "claude_session_id", "label",
    "label_source", "window_name",
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Visibility — real terminal vs popup
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def compute_visibility(session: str, window_id: str, windows, clients) -> dict:
    """Is this target on screen, and is it COVERED by a popup overlay?

    The case this exists for, measured: the operator asked for a window that was
    ALREADY current on the base terminal and merely COVERED by two
    `display-popup` overlays. Nothing in this repo could express the difference
    between "not showing it" and "showing it underneath two popups", so "open
    it" was the wrong action and looked like the right one.

    Definitions, stated so they can be argued with:
      * `visible`  — this window is the ACTIVE window of its session AND some
                     attached client is displaying that session.
      * `covered`  — visible, the client showing it is the BASE TERMINAL, and at
                     least one popup client is attached (a popup overlays the
                     screen). A target shown BY a popup is on top, not covered.
    """
    out = {"clients": None, "attached_clients": None, "base_terminal": None,
           "popups": None, "window_active": None, "visible": None,
           "covered": None, "status": MEASURED_OK}

    if clients is None or windows is None:
        missing = []
        if clients is None:
            missing.append("clients")
        if windows is None:
            missing.append("windows")
        out["status"] = unmeasured("tmux-" + "+".join(missing) + "-unmeasured")
        return out

    active = windows.get((session, window_id))
    if active is None:
        out["status"] = unmeasured(
            f"window-not-in-list-windows:{session}:{window_id}")
        return out

    attached = [c for c in clients if c["session"] == session]
    popups_all = [c for c in clients if c["popup"]]
    base_all = [c for c in clients if c["base_terminal"]]

    out["clients"] = clients
    out["attached_clients"] = attached
    out["base_terminal"] = base_all[0] if base_all else None
    out["popups"] = popups_all
    out["window_active"] = active

    out["visible"] = bool(active and attached)
    shown_by_base = any(c["base_terminal"] for c in attached)
    out["covered"] = bool(out["visible"] and shown_by_base and popups_all)
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# git branch + PR state
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def measure_git(cwd: Optional[str], src: Sources) -> dict:
    """Branch of the cwd, and the open PR for it.

    🔴 Closes session-manager's own documented blind spot: "a window idle because
    its PR is blocked looks exactly like a window idle because the work is
    done." A failed `gh` degrades to UNMEASURED, NEVER to "no PR" — the latter
    would assert the blocked-PR case is absent when it was simply not looked at.
    """
    out = {"branch": None, "branch_status": MEASURED_OK,
           "branch_presence": PRESENCE_UNMEASURED,
           "pr": None, "pr_status": MEASURED_OK,
           "pr_presence": PRESENCE_UNMEASURED}
    if not cwd:
        out["branch_status"] = unmeasured("no-cwd")
        out["pr_status"] = unmeasured("no-cwd")
        return out

    runner = src.git_runner or src.runner
    if cwd in src.branch_cache:
        branch, branch_status, branch_presence = src.branch_cache[cwd]
    else:
        rc, stdout, stderr = runner(
            ["git", "-C", cwd, "branch", "--show-current"], timeout=15)
        if rc != 0:
            branch = None
            branch_status = unmeasured(
                f"git-branch-failed:rc={rc}:{(stderr or '').strip()[:120]}")
            branch_presence = PRESENCE_UNMEASURED
        else:
            name = (stdout or "").strip()
            branch = name or None
            # 🔴 A DETACHED HEAD is a MEASURED absence of a branch: git answered,
            # and there genuinely is no branch. It is ABSENT, not UNMEASURED —
            # calling it unmeasured would send a reader looking for a broken git.
            branch_status = MEASURED_OK
            branch_presence = PRESENCE_PRESENT if name else PRESENCE_ABSENT
        src.branch_cache[cwd] = (branch, branch_status, branch_presence)
    out["branch"] = branch
    out["branch_status"] = branch_status
    out["branch_presence"] = branch_presence

    # 🔴 THE TRICHOTOMY THAT MATTERS MOST. "No open PR for this branch" and "the
    # gh call did not happen" are OPPOSITE answers to "is this window idle
    # because its PR is blocked?", and a shared token makes them indistinguishable
    # — which is precisely session-manager's documented blind spot, reintroduced.
    if not src.want_pr:
        out["pr_status"] = unmeasured("pr-lookup-not-requested")
        return out
    if branch is None:
        out["pr_status"] = unmeasured("no-branch-to-query")
        return out

    cache_key = (cwd, branch)
    if cache_key in src.pr_cache:
        out["pr"], out["pr_status"], out["pr_presence"] = src.pr_cache[cache_key]
        return out

    gh = src.gh_runner or src.runner
    rc, stdout, stderr = gh(
        ["gh", "pr", "list", "--head", branch, "--state", "open",
         "--json", "number,title,state,isDraft,url,mergeStateStatus"],
        timeout=45)
    if rc != 0:
        result = (None, unmeasured(
            f"gh-failed:rc={rc}:{(stderr or '').strip()[:120]}"),
            PRESENCE_UNMEASURED)
    else:
        try:
            prs = json.loads(stdout or "[]")
        except ValueError as exc:
            result = (None, unmeasured(f"gh-bad-json:{exc}"),
                      PRESENCE_UNMEASURED)
        else:
            # gh answered. A NON-EMPTY list is PRESENT; an EMPTY list is a
            # MEASURED absence — "this branch really has no open PR".
            result = (prs, MEASURED_OK,
                      PRESENCE_PRESENT if prs else PRESENCE_ABSENT)
    src.pr_cache[cache_key] = result
    out["pr"], out["pr_status"], out["pr_presence"] = result
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Building targets
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def build_targets(src: Sources) -> dict:
    """Join all four sources on (host, session, window_id) -> targets + coverage."""
    dropped: list = []
    tmux = collect_tmux(src)
    dropped.extend(tmux["dropped"])
    slots, slots_status, _ = load_slots(src)
    registry = load_registry(src)
    dropped.extend(registry["dropped"])
    sm = load_session_manager(src)
    dropped.extend(sm["dropped"])

    panes = tmux["panes"]
    targets: list = []

    if panes is None:
        coverage = _coverage(0, 0, 0, 0, tmux, slots_status, registry, sm)
        return {"targets": targets, "coverage": coverage, "dropped": dropped,
                "slots": slots}

    # One target per WINDOW. A window's first pane supplies cwd/title; extra
    # panes are recorded so a multi-pane window is not silently reduced to one.
    by_window: dict = {}
    for pane in panes:
        key = (pane["session"], pane["window_id"])
        by_window.setdefault(key, []).append(pane)

    n_slot = n_reg = n_sm = 0
    for (session, window_id), pane_list in by_window.items():
        head = pane_list[0]
        slot = slots.get(session)
        if slot:
            n_slot += 1

        reg_matches = registry["by_key"].get((session, window_id)) or []
        harness = None
        harness_status = None
        harness_status_field = MEASURED_OK
        # 🔴 See ALL_PRESENCE_STATES. UNMEASURED = the registry could not be
        # read; ABSENT = it was read and this window has no record (the MAJORITY
        # case, 35 of 59 measured); PRESENT = a record joined.
        harness_presence = PRESENCE_UNMEASURED
        if is_unmeasured(registry["status"]):
            harness_status_field = registry["status"]
        elif reg_matches:
            harness_presence = PRESENCE_PRESENT
            n_reg += 1
            obj, pane_id = reg_matches[0]
            harness = shape_harness(obj, pane_id)
            harness_status = obj.get("status")
            if len(reg_matches) > 1:
                dropped.append(
                    f"{len(reg_matches)} registry records claim "
                    f"{session}:{window_id}; used pid={obj.get('pid')}")
        else:
            # 🔴 A window with NO registry record is NORMAL — 35 of 59 measured.
            # It is a real measured absence, not an error and not a zero.
            harness_presence = PRESENCE_ABSENT
            harness_status_field = MEASURED_OK

        sm_matches = sm["by_key"].get((session, window_id)) or []
        sm_block = {f: None for f in SM_PASSTHROUGH_FIELDS}
        sm_status_field = sm["status"]
        sm_host = None
        # 🔴 The same trichotomy. UNMEASURED = session-manager could not be run
        # or parsed; ABSENT = it ran and reported no row for this window (so
        # waiting_probable is genuinely UNKNOWN for it, never False); PRESENT =
        # a row joined and every field below is session-manager's OWN value.
        sm_presence = PRESENCE_UNMEASURED
        if is_unmeasured(sm["status"]):
            pass
        elif sm_matches:
            sm_presence = PRESENCE_PRESENT
            n_sm += 1
            sm_host, row = sm_matches[0]
            for f in SM_PASSTHROUGH_FIELDS:
                sm_block[f] = row.get(f)
            sm_status_field = MEASURED_OK
        else:
            sm_presence = PRESENCE_ABSENT
            sm_status_field = unmeasured("no-session-manager-row-for-window")

        label, label_source = _label_for(slot, head.get("cwd"), session)
        vis = compute_visibility(session, window_id, tmux["windows"],
                                 tmux["clients"])
        git = measure_git(head.get("cwd"), src)

        targets.append({
            "host": src.host,
            "session": session,
            "window_id": window_id,
            "window_index": head.get("window_index"),
            "pane_id": head.get("pane_id"),
            "panes": [p["pane_id"] for p in pane_list],
            "cwd": head.get("cwd"),
            "repo": os.path.basename(head["cwd"]) if head.get("cwd") else None,
            "pane_title": head.get("pane_title"),
            "address": f"{session}:{window_id}",

            "label": label,
            "label_source": label_source,
            "codename": slot["codename"] if slot else None,
            "hotkey": slot["hotkey"] if slot else None,
            "colour": slot["colour"] if slot else None,
            "slot_status": slots_status,

            # 🔴 TWO STATUSES, NEVER COLLAPSED. Distinct names, no mapping.
            "harness_status": harness_status,
            "harness_status_measurement": harness_status_field,
            "harness_presence": harness_presence,
            "harness": harness,

            "waiting_probable": sm_block["waiting_probable"],
            "waiting_signals": sm_block["waiting_signals"],
            "waiting_status": sm_block["waiting_status"],
            "session_manager_status": sm_block["status"],
            "session_manager_measurement": sm_status_field,
            "session_manager_presence": sm_presence,
            "session_manager_host": sm_host,
            "unsent_prompt": sm_block["unsent_prompt"],
            "unsent_prompt_status": sm_block["unsent_prompt_status"],
            "age_secs": sm_block["age_secs"],
            "age_source": sm_block["age_source"],
            "runtime": sm_block["runtime"],
            "task": sm_block["task"],
            "claude_session_id": sm_block["claude_session_id"],

            "visibility": vis,
            "git": git,
        })

    coverage = _coverage(len(targets), n_slot, n_reg, n_sm, tmux,
                         slots_status, registry, sm)
    return {"targets": targets, "coverage": coverage, "dropped": dropped,
            "slots": slots}


def _label_for(slot, cwd, session) -> tuple:
    """🔴 A session with no slot entry has NO codename and NO hotkey. The label
    falls back to the cwd basename, then to the session name. None is invented.
    """
    if slot and slot.get("codename"):
        return slot["codename"], LABEL_SOURCE_SLOT
    if cwd:
        return os.path.basename(cwd), LABEL_SOURCE_CWD
    return session, LABEL_SOURCE_SESSION


def _coverage(n_windows, n_slot, n_reg, n_sm, tmux, slots_status, registry, sm):
    """🔴 Coverage is REPORTED, not asserted. 'Registry matched 24 of 59
    windows' is the honest statement; a bare 24 hides the denominator and a
    silent 0 for the other 35 would invent an absence."""
    return {
        "windows_total": n_windows,
        "tmux_panes_status": tmux["panes_status"],
        "tmux_windows_status": tmux["windows_status"],
        "tmux_clients_status": tmux["clients_status"],
        "slot_table_status": slots_status,
        "windows_with_slot": n_slot,
        "windows_without_slot": n_windows - n_slot,
        "registry_status": registry["status"],
        "registry_records": len(registry["records"]),
        "registry_records_with_tmux": registry["with_tmux"],
        "registry_unparseable_files": registry["unparseable"],
        "windows_with_registry_match": n_reg,
        "windows_without_registry_match": n_windows - n_reg,
        "session_manager_status": sm["status"],
        "session_manager_rows": sm["rows_total"],
        "session_manager_rows_unjoinable": sm["rows_unjoinable"],
        "windows_with_session_manager_row": n_sm,
        "windows_without_session_manager_row": n_windows - n_sm,
    }


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Selector matching
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def normalise_hotkey(selector: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """`Alt+Shift+G` / `$mod+Shift+G` / `G` -> `G`; anything else -> None.

    🔴 CASE IS PRESERVED. `g` is grove (scratch) and `G` is Gold (scratch2);
    lower-casing the key would merge ten pairs of distinct slots.
    """
    if not selector:
        return None
    tail = selector.split(HOTKEY_SEPARATOR)[-1].strip()
    return tail if len(tail) == 1 else None


def match_kinds(selector: str, target: dict) -> list:
    """Which selector KINDS does `selector` match this target under?

    All kinds are tried and the matches are UNIONED. A selector that names two
    different targets under two different kinds is ambiguous, and saying which
    kinds fired is what makes the refusal actionable.
    """
    kinds = []
    sel = (selector or "").strip()
    if not sel:
        return kinds

    codename = target.get("codename")
    if codename and sel.lower() == codename.lower():
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_CODENAME)

    key = normalise_hotkey(sel)
    if key is not None and target.get("hotkey") and key == target["hotkey"]:
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_HOTKEY)

    if ":" in sel:
        head, tail = sel.split(":", 1)
        if head == target["session"]:
            if tail.startswith("@") and tail == target["window_id"]:
                kinds.append(SELECTOR_ADDRESS)
            elif tail.isdigit() and tail == str(target.get("window_index")):
                kinds.append(SELECTOR_SESSION_INDEX)

    if sel.startswith("@") and sel == target["window_id"]:
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_WINDOW_ID)

    harness = target.get("harness") or {}
    if harness.get("name") and sel == harness["name"]:
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_HARNESS_NAME)

    if sel == target["session"]:
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_SESSION)

    if target.get("cwd") and (sel == target["cwd"] or sel == target.get("repo")):
        kinds.append(SELECTOR_CWD)

    return kinds


def _brief(target: dict, kinds) -> dict:
    """The candidate line shown when a selector is ambiguous — enough to pick."""
    return {
        "address": target["address"],
        "host": target["host"],
        "session": target["session"],
        "window_id": target["window_id"],
        "window_index": target["window_index"],
        "label": target["label"],
        "label_source": target["label_source"],
        "codename": target["codename"],
        "hotkey": target["hotkey"],
        "cwd": target["cwd"],
        "harness_status": target["harness_status"],
        "waiting_probable": target["waiting_probable"],
        "matched_kinds": kinds,
    }


def resolve(selector: str, src: Optional[Sources] = None,
            built: Optional[dict] = None) -> dict:
    """Resolve `selector` to exactly ONE target, or refuse.

    🔴 AMBIGUITY IS A FIRST-CLASS OUTCOME. More than one match returns
    STATUS_AMBIGUOUS with every candidate listed. It NEVER picks the first —
    silently picking is how an operator's "open Gold" lands in the wrong window
    and nobody finds out.
    """
    src = src or Sources()
    built = built if built is not None else build_targets(src)
    targets = built["targets"]

    matches = []
    for t in targets:
        kinds = match_kinds(selector, t)
        if kinds:
            matches.append((t, kinds))

    result = {
        "selector": selector,
        "selector_kinds_supported": list(ALL_SELECTOR_KINDS),
        "sources_consulted": list(ALL_SOURCES),
        "coverage": built["coverage"],
        "dropped": built["dropped"],
        "target": None,
        "candidates": [],
        "candidate_count": len(matches),
    }

    if not matches:
        result["status"] = STATUS_UNMATCHED
        result["reason"] = (
            f"no target matched {selector!r} under any of "
            f"{len(ALL_SELECTOR_KINDS)} selector kinds; consulted "
            f"{', '.join(ALL_SOURCES)} "
            f"({built['coverage']['windows_total']} window(s) known)")
        return result

    if len(matches) > 1:
        result["status"] = STATUS_AMBIGUOUS
        result["candidates"] = [_brief(t, k) for t, k in matches]
        result["reason"] = (
            f"{len(matches)} targets match {selector!r} — refusing to pick. "
            f"Disambiguate with an address (session:@window_id) or "
            f"session:index.")
        return result

    target, kinds = matches[0]
    result["status"] = STATUS_RESOLVED
    result["matched_kinds"] = kinds
    result["target"] = target
    return result


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Rendering
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
#: The key holding the signal's NAME inside a session-manager waiting signal.
SIGNAL_NAME_KEY = "signal"
#: 🔴 The key holding the raw CAPTURED PANE LINE. Measured shape of one signal:
#: `{"line": "<text scraped off the operator's screen>", "signal":
#: "selection_menu"}`. This repo is PUBLIC and forbids captured text in any
#: committed artifact, and a rendered line lands in scrollback, logs and pasted
#: PR bodies. `signal_names` therefore projects the NAME ONLY and this key is
#: named here so the omission is deliberate and greppable rather than an
#: oversight. The full signal object is still present in `--json` for a caller
#: that genuinely needs it and knows where the output is going.
SIGNAL_CAPTURED_TEXT_KEY = "line"


def signal_names(signals) -> list:
    """Project session-manager waiting signals to their NAMES.

    Tolerates both shapes because the field is another tool's output, not ours:
    a dict yields `signal[SIGNAL_NAME_KEY]`, a bare string yields itself. It
    NEVER yields `SIGNAL_CAPTURED_TEXT_KEY` — see that constant.
    """
    out = []
    for s in signals or []:
        if isinstance(s, dict):
            out.append(str(s.get(SIGNAL_NAME_KEY) or "unnamed-signal"))
        else:
            out.append(str(s))
    return out


def _fmt(value, status=None):
    if status is not None and is_unmeasured(status):
        return f"UNMEASURED ({status.split(':', 1)[1]})"
    if value is None:
        return "UNMEASURED (no value and no reason recorded)"
    return str(value)


#: The exact rendered strings for the three harness states. Pinned as constants
#: because "no record" and "not measured" are the two the operator must never
#: confuse, and a whole-string assertion on a constant is the only guard a
#: reword cannot walk.
HARNESS_NO_RECORD = ("no harness record for this window "
                     "(MEASURED absence — normal, most windows have none)")
WAITING_NOT_MEASURED = "UNMEASURED (session-manager reported no value)"
#: 🔴 The two PR strings that must never merge. `gh` ran and found nothing vs
#: `gh` never ran. Pinned as whole strings so a reword cannot walk the guard.
PR_NONE_MEASURED = "none — gh answered, this branch has NO open PR (MEASURED)"
BRANCH_DETACHED = "none — detached HEAD, no branch (MEASURED)"


def render_harness_status(t: dict) -> str:
    """🔴 Three states, three distinct strings. Never collapses the middle one
    into the UNMEASURED wording — a window with no harness record has been
    measured, and saying otherwise would send an operator hunting a broken
    registry that is working perfectly."""
    if t["harness_presence"] == PRESENCE_UNMEASURED:
        return _fmt(None, t["harness_status_measurement"])
    if t["harness_presence"] == PRESENCE_ABSENT:
        return HARNESS_NO_RECORD
    if t["harness_status"] is None:
        return _fmt(None, unmeasured("registry-record-has-no-status-field"))
    return str(t["harness_status"])


def render_waiting_probable(t: dict) -> str:
    """session-manager's value, or its OWN reason for not having one. Never
    renders a missing waiting_probable as False — `--no-capture` makes it null
    for every row, and False would assert 'measured, not waiting'."""
    if t["session_manager_presence"] != PRESENCE_PRESENT:
        return _fmt(None, t["session_manager_measurement"])
    if t["waiting_probable"] is None:
        detail = t.get("waiting_status") or "no reason given"
        return f"{WAITING_NOT_MEASURED} [{detail}]"
    return str(t["waiting_probable"])


def render(result: dict) -> str:
    lines = []
    status = result["status"]
    sel = result["selector"]

    if status == STATUS_UNMATCHED:
        lines.append(f"UNMATCHED  {sel!r}")
        lines.append(f"  {result['reason']}")
        lines.append("  sources consulted: " +
                     ", ".join(result["sources_consulted"]))
        cov = result["coverage"]
        lines.append(f"    tmux           : {cov['tmux_panes_status']} "
                     f"({cov['windows_total']} windows)")
        lines.append(f"    slot table     : {cov['slot_table_status']}")
        lines.append(f"    registry       : {cov['registry_status']} "
                     f"({cov['registry_records_with_tmux']} addressed records)")
        lines.append(f"    session-manager: {cov['session_manager_status']} "
                     f"({cov['session_manager_rows']} rows)")
    elif status == STATUS_AMBIGUOUS:
        lines.append(f"AMBIGUOUS  {sel!r} -> {result['candidate_count']} targets")
        lines.append(f"  {result['reason']}")
        for c in result["candidates"]:
            lines.append(
                f"    {c['address']:<24} idx={c['window_index']:<3} "
                f"{c['label']:<22} matched={','.join(c['matched_kinds'])}")
    else:
        t = result["target"]
        lines.append(f"RESOLVED   {sel!r} -> {t['address']}  "
                     f"(matched: {', '.join(result['matched_kinds'])})")
        lines.append(f"  host/session   : {t['host']} / {t['session']}")
        lines.append(f"  window         : {t['window_id']} "
                     f"(index {t['window_index']}, panes "
                     f"{', '.join(t['panes'])})")
        lines.append(f"  label          : {t['label']}  "
                     f"(source: {t['label_source']})")
        lines.append(f"  codename/hotkey: "
                     f"{t['codename'] or 'NONE (no slot entry)'} / "
                     f"{t['hotkey'] or 'NONE (no slot entry)'}")
        lines.append(f"  cwd            : {t['cwd'] or 'UNMEASURED'}")
        lines.append(f"  task           : {t['task'] or '-'}")
        lines.append("")
        lines.append("  -- two statuses, never collapsed --")
        lines.append(f"  harness_status : {render_harness_status(t)}"
                     f"   [registry: waiting on a turn]")
        lines.append(f"  waiting_probable: {render_waiting_probable(t)}"
                     f"   [session-manager: pane text looks like a question]")
        if t["waiting_signals"]:
            lines.append(f"    signals      : "
                         f"{', '.join(signal_names(t['waiting_signals']))}")
        lines.append(f"  unsent_prompt  : {t['unsent_prompt'] or '-'}")
        lines.append(f"  age            : {_fmt(t['age_secs'])}s "
                     f"(source: {t['age_source'] or 'UNMEASURED'})")
        lines.append("")
        vis = t["visibility"]
        lines.append("  -- terminal vs popup --")
        if is_unmeasured(vis["status"]):
            lines.append(f"  visibility     : {_fmt(None, vis['status'])}")
        else:
            lines.append(f"  window_active  : {vis['window_active']}")
            lines.append(f"  visible        : {vis['visible']}")
            lines.append(f"  covered        : {vis['covered']}"
                         + ("   <- ON SCREEN BUT UNDER A POPUP"
                            if vis["covered"] else ""))
            for c in vis["clients"]:
                role = "POPUP " if c["popup"] else "BASE  "
                here = " *attached to this session*" \
                    if c["session"] == t["session"] else ""
                lines.append(f"    {role} {c['tty']:<12} {c['term']:<16} "
                             f"{c['size']:<10} sess={c['session']}{here}")
        lines.append("")
        g = t["git"]
        lines.append("  -- git / PR --")
        if g["branch_presence"] == PRESENCE_ABSENT:
            lines.append(f"  branch         : {BRANCH_DETACHED}")
        else:
            lines.append(
                f"  branch         : {_fmt(g['branch'], g['branch_status'])}")
        if g["pr_presence"] == PRESENCE_UNMEASURED:
            lines.append(f"  open PR        : {_fmt(None, g['pr_status'])}")
        elif g["pr_presence"] == PRESENCE_ABSENT:
            lines.append(f"  open PR        : {PR_NONE_MEASURED}")
        else:
            for pr in g["pr"]:
                lines.append(f"  open PR        : #{pr.get('number')} "
                             f"{pr.get('mergeStateStatus') or ''} "
                             f"{'DRAFT ' if pr.get('isDraft') else ''}"
                             f"{pr.get('title') or ''}")

    cov = result["coverage"]
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("  -- coverage (a window with no match is normal, not a zero) --")
    lines.append(f"  windows                     : {cov['windows_total']}")
    lines.append(f"  with slot / without         : {cov['windows_with_slot']}"
                 f" / {cov['windows_without_slot']}")
    lines.append(f"  with registry match / without: "
                 f"{cov['windows_with_registry_match']}"
                 f" / {cov['windows_without_registry_match']}")
    lines.append(f"  with session-manager / without: "
                 f"{cov['windows_with_session_manager_row']}"
                 f" / {cov['windows_without_session_manager_row']}")
    if cov["session_manager_rows_unjoinable"]:
        lines.append(f"  🔴 session-manager rows NOT joined: "
                     f"{cov['session_manager_rows_unjoinable']}")
    for d in result["dropped"]:
        lines.append(f"  DROPPED: {d}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
EXIT_RESOLVED = 0
EXIT_AMBIGUOUS = 2
EXIT_UNMATCHED = 3

_EXIT_FOR = {STATUS_RESOLVED: EXIT_RESOLVED,
             STATUS_AMBIGUOUS: EXIT_AMBIGUOUS,
             STATUS_UNMATCHED: EXIT_UNMATCHED}


def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="session-resolve",
        description="Resolve a selector to ONE tmux/harness target. READ-ONLY.")
    p.add_argument("command", choices=("show", "resolve"),
                   help="both print the resolved target; `resolve` is an alias")
    p.add_argument("selector",
                   help="codename (Gold) | hotkey (G, Alt+Shift+G) | "
                        "session:index (scratch2:2) | address (scratch2:@81) | "
                        "window id (@81) | harness name | cwd/repo")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="JSON output")
    p.add_argument("--host", default="workbench")
    p.add_argument("--pr", action="store_true",
                   help="also query `gh pr list` for each cwd's branch "
                        "(slow; degrades to UNMEASURED, never to 'no PR')")
    p.add_argument("--registry-dir", default=DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR)
    p.add_argument("--slot-table", default=DEFAULT_SLOT_TABLE)
    return p


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    src = Sources(host=args.host, registry_dir=args.registry_dir,
                  slot_table_path=args.slot_table, want_pr=args.pr)
    result = resolve(args.selector, src)
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2, sort_keys=True, default=str))
    else:
        print(render(result))
    return _EXIT_FOR[result["status"]]


if __name__ == "__main__":  # pragma: no cover
    sys.exit(main())
